{
 "id": "l11",
 "label": "11",
 "title": "Making comparisons",
 "summary": "As … as (kasíng-, magkasíng-), like (parého, gáya), more than (mas … káysa), very (nápaka-) and most (pinaká-)",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Say that A is as [adjective] as B with kasíng-",
  "Say that A and B are equally [adjective] with magkasíng-",
  "Choose kasíng-, kasín- or kasím- from the first letter of the root",
  "Say that A is more [adjective] than B, or not as [adjective] as B",
  "Say very [adjective] and the most [adjective]"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Comparing qualities",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "People and things have qualities that can be compared. A quality is expressed by an **adjective** (Lesson 4), which can show whether two people or things are **equal** or **not equal**, or whether a person or thing has a **high degree** of a quality, perhaps the highest of all."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "As in English, Tagalog has several ways of comparing things at different levels or degrees."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Level",
      "English pattern",
      "Tagalog tool"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Equality (A = B)",
       "A is as … as B; A and B are equally …; A is … like B",
       "[[kasíng-|other]], [[magkasíng-|other]], [[parého|other]], [[katúlad|other]], [[gáya|other]]"
      ],
      [
       "Inequality (A ≠ B)",
       "A is more … than B; A is not as … as B",
       "[[mas|other]] / [[lalóng|other]] / [[higít na|other]] + [[káysa|other]]; [[hindi kasíng-|other]]"
      ],
      [
       "Intensive",
       "A is very …",
       "doubling + ligature; [[nápaka-|other]]"
      ],
      [
       "Superlative",
       "A is the most …",
       "[[pinaká-|other]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The four levels"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Equality: as … as",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To say that two nouns (A and B) are equal in one quality, either attach the prefix **kasíng-** or **magkasíng-** to the **root word** of an adjective, or use the words **parého, katúlad** or **gáya**. On their own, kasíng- and magkasíng- mean *as*; parého, katúlad and gáya mean *similar to* or *like*."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. A is as [adjective] as B"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "A + kasíng- adjective + ni (or ng) + B",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Example",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Si Maria",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ay",
         "r": "ay"
        },
        {
         "t": "kasínggandá",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "ni",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "Elena",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Maria is as beautiful as Elena."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "This phrase means *as + adjective + as*. The order can also be turned around, with **kasíng- + adjective** at the beginning of the sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Si Maria ay kasínggandá ni Elena.",
       "en": "Maria is as beautiful as Elena.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Si",
         "article (name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Maria",
         "Maria",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "kasínggandá",
         "as beautiful as",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ni",
         "of (name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Elena",
         "Elena",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Kasínggalíng ni Rizal si Bonifacio.",
       "en": "As good as Rizal, Bonifacio is. (lit.) Bonifacio is as good as Rizal.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kasínggalíng",
         "as good as",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ni",
         "of (name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Rizal",
         "Rizal",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "article (name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Bonifacio",
         "Bonifacio",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "2. A and B are equally [adjective]"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "With **magkasíng- + adjective**, the two subjects being compared are mentioned **side by side**, instead of one on each side of the adjective as in formula 1. When magkasíng- begins the sentence, the name or names come after it, introduced by an article (sina) or replaced by a pronoun (kamí)."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Sina Maria at Elena ay magkasínggandá.",
       "en": "Maria and Elena are equally beautiful.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Sina",
         "article (2+ names)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Maria",
         "Maria",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Elena",
         "Elena",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "magkasínggandá",
         "equally beautiful",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Magkasínggalíng sina Rizal at Bonifacio.",
       "en": "Rizal and Bonifacio are equally good.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magkasínggalíng",
         "equally good",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "sina",
         "article (2+ names)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Rizal",
         "Rizal",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Bonifacio",
         "Bonifacio",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Magkasíntaás kamí.",
       "en": "We are equally tall.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magkasíntaás",
         "equally tall",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kamí",
         "we",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Articles with names",
     "t": "Names of people need the articles **ni/nina** and **si/sina**. Names of things or places use **ng/ng mga**."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Kasíng-, kasín- or kasím-?",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The ending of the prefix changes to suit the **first letter of the root word**, much like the ligatures in Lesson 7."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "The root begins with…",
      "Prefix",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "a, e, i, o, u, k, g, h, m, n, w, y",
       "[[kasíng-|other]] or [[magkasíng-|other]]",
       "**kasíngkulót**  as curly as"
      ],
      [
       "d, l, r, s, t",
       "[[kasín-|other]] or [[magkasín-|other]]",
       "**kasíndumí**  as dirty as"
      ],
      [
       "b, p",
       "[[kasím-|other]] or [[magkasím-|other]]",
       "**magkasímbahò**  as foul-smelling as"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Three forms of the prefix"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "You have already met examples of each: kasín**g**gandá and kasín**g**galíng (g), magkasín**t**aás and kasín**d**únong (t, d), kasím**b**aít (b)."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "equal",
     "title": "Words for equality"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
