  {
   "title": "Very: the intensive",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The intensive degree describes a quality more forcefully, like a phrase starting with *very*. There are many ways to do this in Tagalog; these two are the most common."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. Double the adjective and add a ligature"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Repeat the adjective and join the two with the right ligature, **-ng, -g** or **na** (Lesson 7)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Tagalog",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[magandáng-magandá|pred]]",
       "very beautiful"
      ],
      [
       "[[matandáng-matandâ|pred]]",
       "very old"
      ],
      [
       "[[pángit na pángit|pred]]",
       "very ugly"
      ],
      [
       "[[masípag na masípag|pred]]",
       "very industrious"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Doubling"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "2. Add the prefix nápaka-"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The prefix **nápaka-** means *very*."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Tagalog",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[nápakapangit|pred]]",
       "very ugly"
      ],
      [
       "[[nápakasipag|pred]]",
       "very industrious"
      ],
      [
       "[[nápakatandâ|pred]]",
       "very old"
      ],
      [
       "[[nápakagandá|pred]]",
       "very beautiful"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Nápaka-"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The most: the superlative",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The superlative expresses the highest degree of a quality (the greatest, the best, the most). It uses the prefix **pinaká-**, which means *most*, attached to adjectives."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
